# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTES (not part of the diff -- read before applying)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Same issue and fix as phase1_warmup_v2.patch, applied to phase2.py's
# already basis_gates-fixed workers: every measurement here still runs in a
# brand-new multiprocessing.Process (Windows: 'spawn', so a full
# re-execution each time), and transpile()'s first call in a fresh process
# pays a real, one-time warmup cost -- confirmed directly at 2.41s (no
# warmup) vs. 0.07s (with warmup) for an identical 156-qubit circuit, a 32x
# difference from measurement methodology alone.
#
# This project's own sandbox (Python stand-in for the Rust core, so
# absolute numbers don't transfer -- only the structure does) found that
# applying this fix symmetrically to BOTH workers collapses phase2.py's
# already-reported 5.5x-42x speedups (156-1000 qubits) down to roughly
# 0.7x-1.7x -- i.e. close to parity, and briefly reversed at one size
# tested:
#
#     qubits | qiskit (warm) | psf (warm, stand-in core) | ratio
#     156    | 0.2181s       | 0.1302s                    | 1.7x
#     300    | 0.2953s       | 0.2202s                    | 1.3x
#     500    | 0.2225s       | 0.3242s                    | 0.7x (Qiskit faster)
#     1000   | 0.7272s       | 0.7425s                    | 1.0x
#
# Re-run this patched version (and phase1_warmup_v2.patch's patched
# phase1.py) on the real Rust core for the numbers that should actually
# replace section 4's "203x -> 4.4x" curve.
